---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="About | Sakha Wibisono"
    description="From a clinic records app to SSO at a state-owned enterprise. Here is how I have been learning to build software that people actually use."
>
    <main id="content" class="shell page space-y-8">
        <header class="page-heading">
            <p class="eyebrow">Beyond the resume</p><h1>A little about me.</h1>
        </header>
        <section
            class="glass-card grid gap-8 p-7 sm:p-10 md:grid-cols-[1.5fr_1fr]"
            aria-labelledby="story-title"
        >
            <div class="space-y-5">
                <h2 id="story-title" class="sr-only">My story</h2>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    My name is Sakha. I graduated from the D3 Software
                    Application Engineering program at Telkom University in
                    2024, then continued straight into the S1 Informatics
                    program at the same campus.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    My diploma thesis was MedRecordX, a patient medical record
                    application built for Telagasari Azimat Clinic. Small
                    project by most measures, but it was the first time I had to
                    build something that real people would rely on every single
                    working day. That shifts how you think about error handling.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    During my D3 program, I interned at Directorate PuTI Telkom
                    University as a frontend developer, helping build and
                    maintain the university's internal academic information
                    system using AngularJS. Part of that work involved
                    integrating the T-Feeder application with Neo Feeder
                    PDDIKTI, which meant coordinating across backend teams and
                    understanding how API-based data synchronization actually
                    holds up at institutional scale.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    Then I joined the Asah Ied by Dicoding cohort. This one ran
                    closer to a real job than any training program I had been
                    through. I led the React frontend and handled backend
                    integration for a predictive lead scoring app in a banking
                    context. The backend ran on Supabase. I managed database
                    design, authentication, authorization, and the connection to
                    the ML model. We worked in Agile sprints, and almost every
                    sprint had something go sideways. That turned out to be
                    where most of the real learning happened.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    Most recently, I interned at Perum Peruri in Jakarta, where
                    I built the SSO frontend module using Astro and Vue.js with
                    a hybrid SSR and SPA architecture. That meant properly
                    implementing OAuth 2.0 and OpenID Connect, including token
                    exchange, refresh logic, and all the access protection edge
                    cases that tutorials tend to gloss over. It was the first
                    project where I genuinely felt the weight of getting
                    authentication wrong.
                </p>
                <p class="text-muted">
                    My bachelor's thesis looks at using generative AI for
                    process discovery in operational maintenance data at an
                    electric power company. That work pulled me toward data
                    science and process mining more seriously than any
                    coursework had. Right now I am open to roles on the data
                    side of things too, whether that is IT Business Analyst,
                    Business Intelligence, or something else in that space.
                    Still finding my spark, but the direction is starting to get
                    clearer.
                </p>
            </div>
            <aside
                class="space-y-7 border-t border-line pt-7 md:border-t-0 md:border-l md:pt-0 md:pl-8"
                aria-label="At a glance"
            >
                <svg class="icon text-accent" aria-hidden="true"
                    ><use href="/icons/tabler.svg#school"></use></svg
                >
                <div>
                    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">2024 - 2026</p><h3
                        class="font-medium"
                    >
                        Bachelor of Informatics
                    </h3><p class="text-sm text-muted">
                        Telkom University GPA 3.50/4.00
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <p class="eyebrow mb-2">2021 - 2024</p><h3
                        class="font-medium"
                    >
                        Diploma in Software Application Engineering
                    </h3><p class="text-sm text-muted">
                        Telkom University GPA 3.67/4.00
                    </p>
                </div>
                <a href="/education" class="text-link"
                    >My education <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"
                        ><use href="/icons/tabler.svg#arrow-right"></use></svg
                    ></a
                >
            </aside>
        </section>
        <div class="grid gap-5 md:grid-cols-2">
            <section class="glass-card p-7 sm:p-9">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">
                    <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"
                        ><use href="/icons/tabler.svg#code"></use></svg
                    >What I'm working on now
                </p><h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-medium">
                    Interfaces that don't make people think twice.
                </h2><div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-muted">
                    <p>
                        Responsive layouts, reusable components, and clean API
                        integration are the parts of web development I keep
                        coming back to. But what has been pulling my attention
                        lately is the space between the interface and the data
                        behind it. How they talk to each other, and what breaks
                        when they stop doing that well.
                    </p><p>
                        Authentication taught me a lot about that. So did trying
                        to make a language model do something genuinely useful
                        with unstructured industrial logs. Both problems look
                        manageable from the outside and get complicated fast
                        once you are actually inside them.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>
            <section class="glass-card p-7 sm:p-9">
                <p class="eyebrow mb-4">
                    <svg class="icon" aria-hidden="true"
                        ><use href="/icons/tabler.svg#briefcase"></use></svg
                    >Outside the project work
                </p><h2 class="mb-4 text-xl font-medium">
                    Learning with other people.
                </h2><div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-muted">
                    <p>
                        At Chevalier Lab I joined a Laravel study group. Laravel
                        was not my main stack at the time, but learning it
                        alongside other people in a structured setting showed me
                        how much faster things click when you can ask a question
                        out loud and hear someone else's reasoning in return.
                    </p><p>
                        In HIMA RPLA I served in the advocacy department for D3
                        Application Software Engineering students. The work was
                        mostly listening. Students would come in with a
                        complaint, and learning to figure out what they actually
                        needed versus what they said they needed turned out to
                        be its own kind of skill.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </section>
        </div>
    </main>
</Layout>
